title: Algorithm – State Machine

files:
  - em.prog:em.benchmark.coremark/StateBench.em.ts
  - .assets/img-102-04-01.png

steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]%[cu]StateBench% Algorithm}

      We turn now to the second **CoreMark** algorithm &ndash; a *state-machine* which scans for different forms of numeric text.

      The {[cu]StateBench} module closely tracks `core_state.c`, while also implementing the {[cu]BenchAlgI} interface.

      {[in]By design, %[cu]StateBench% features "low-level" <b>EMS</b> pointer idioms similar to those found in legacy C code.}

  - cmds:
      - close 2
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 7
      - mark 1 B 9
      - mark 1 C 13
      - mark 1 D 16
      - mark 1 E 29
      - mark 1 F 34
    focus: [1, 11]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]%[cu]StateBench% &ndash; static declarations}

      {[cu]StateBench} implements a finite-state-machine which repeatedly scans {[cn]membuf} {[bm,F]} for different numeric text.

      {[in]The %[cn]mem_size% config %[bm,A]% determines the buffer's size.}

      The {[ct]State} type {[bm,B]} enumerates the nodes comprising the underlying state-machine scanner.

      The {[ct]StateCnt} type {[bm,C]} defines a vector used to count oc&shy;curences of each state encountered when scanning.

      Finally, {[cu]StateBench}.{[cf]setup} will consume {[cs]$table} {[bm,D]} and {[cs]$config} {[bm,E]} values when preparing {[cn]membuf} at run-time.

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 69
      - mark 1 B 72
      - mark 1 C 76
    focus: [1, 71]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]%[cu]StateBench% &ndash; %[cf]run% details}

      Local {[ct]StateCnt} vectors allocated at {[bm,A]} hold tallies of fi&shy;nal and transient states encountered while scanning.

      The {[cn]crc} variable at {[bm,C]} combines all of these accumulated counts into a single {[ct]sum_t} returned by {[cu]StateBench}.{[cf]run}.

      Line {[bm,B]} calls {[cf]scan} twice to apply the state-machine to the {[cn]membuf} text while tallying the states encountered.

      {[in]The %[cf]scramble% function purposely "corrupts" the %[cn]membuf% text to force entry into the %[cn]INVALID% state.}

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 222
      - mark 1 B 225
      - mark 1 C 227
    focus: [1, 221]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]%[cu]StateBench% &ndash; %[cf]scan% details}

      The {[cf]scan} function receives our {[ct]StateCnt} vectors as argu&shy;ments &ndash; accessed here via the generic {[ct]index_t} type.

      Using a pointer {[bm,A]} to iterate through {[cn]membuf}, {[cf]nextState} {[bm,B]} matches numeric text at the current {[cn]str} position.

      Note that {[cf]nextState} receives {[cn]cnt} by *reference* &ndash; enab&shy;ling us to update {[cn]str} ahead of the next call to this helper.

      Line {[bm,C]} then increments the {[cn]final_cnt} for the {[ct]State} value returned by {[cf]nextState}.

      {[in]Incrementing the %[cn]cnt% variable here has no apparent purpose; the original %[cx]code_state.c% does the same.}

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 132
      - mark 1 B 134
      - mark 1 C 141
      - mark 1 D 213
    focus: [1, 135]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]%[cu]StateBench% &ndash; %[cf]nextState% details}

      The {[cf]nextState} function first unpacks its {[cn]str_ref} argu&shy;ment into a local {[cn]str} pointer at line {[bm,A]}.

      The loop at {[bm,B]} then advances the {[cn]str} pointer through the underlying buffer until we find matching numeric text.

      {[in]The <b>EMS</b> operators .%[ct]$$% and .%[cs]$inc% applied here to %[cn]str% correspond to %[cx]*str% and %[cx]str++% expressions in C.}

      Inside the loop you'll find a conventional {[ck]switch} {[ck]case} pattern {[bm,C]} used when implementing state-machines.

      Scrolling down to line {[bm,D]}, {[cf]nextState} returns the latest {[ct]State} value after updating its {[cn]str_ref} argument.

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 85
      - mark 1 B 90
      - mark 1 C 100
    focus: [1, 88]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]%[cu]StateBench% &ndash; %[cf]setup% details}

      Once again, **CoreMark** requires us to initialize algorithm datasets using a {[ct]seed_t} value read at *run-time* {[bm,A]}.

      The {[ck]switch} {[ck]case} at line {[bm,C]} uses the local {[cn]seed} variable to select {[ct]text_t} elements from static {[cs]$table} objects.

      The loop at {[bm,B]} then populates {[cn]membuf} using pointer {[cn]p}, copying the text currently bound to the {[cn]pat} variable.

      {[in]The <b>EMS</b> operators&thinsp;%[ct]c$%and %[ct]t$% followed by%[cs]`text`%cor&shy;respond to %[cx]char% and %[cx]string% literals in standard C.}

  - cmds:
      - open 1
    focus: [1, 52]
    text: |
      {[ht,$extra]Extra Credit}

      {[bi,task_alt]} Find {[cu]StateBench} helpers inside {[cx]core_state.c}.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Compare helper functions to sharpen your **EMS** skills.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Call {[cu]StateBench}.{[cf]print} to inspect interim results.

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,$done]Next Steps}

      Two down, one to go....

      We'll now move on to {[cu]ListBench} and its implementa&shy;tion of the algorithm codified in {[cx]core_list_join.c}.


      {[hc]}
